    <!-- https://w3id.org/aief/AIAAIC_071 -->


    <owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="https://w3id.org/aief/AIAAIC_071">
        <rdf:type rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/aief/Incident"/>
        <demonstratesRisk rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/aief/Discrimination"/>
        <demonstratesRisk rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/aief/PrivacyBreach"/>
        <impactsRight rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/aief/Art21_NonDiscrimination"/>
        <impactsRight rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/aief/Art48_PresumptionOfInnocence"/>
        <impactsRight rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/aief/Art8_DataProtection"/>
        <aiCapability>Reasoning (Risk Scoring)</aiCapability>
        <incidentID>AIAAIC-071</incidentID>
        <incidentTitle>SyRI Welfare Fraud Detection System Ruled Unlawful (Netherlands)</incidentTitle>
        <incidentYear>2020</incidentYear>
        <noteText>The Hague District Court (NJCM c.s. v. The Netherlands, ECLI:NL:RBDHA:2020:1878) ruled the System Risk Indication (SyRI) algorithm, used to flag welfare recipients as fraud-risk suspects on unspecified profiling criteria, violated Article 8 ECHR, in part because opaque risk-flagging of citizens as suspects prior to any finding of wrongdoing was incompatible with a fair balance between fraud prevention and privacy/non-discrimination rights.</noteText>
    </owl:NamedIndividual>
    


    <!-- https://w3id.org/aief/AIAAIC_072 -->


    <owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="https://w3id.org/aief/AIAAIC_072">
        <rdf:type rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/aief/Incident"/>
        <demonstratesRisk rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/aief/Discrimination"/>
        <impactsRight rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/aief/Art21_NonDiscrimination"/>
        <impactsRight rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/aief/Art48_PresumptionOfInnocence"/>
        <impactsRight rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/aief/Art6_RightToLiberty"/>
        <aiCapability>Reasoning (Predictive Policing)</aiCapability>
        <incidentID>AIAAIC-072</incidentID>
        <incidentTitle>Predictive Policing Programs Discontinued After Bias Findings (US)</incidentTitle>
        <incidentYear>2020</incidentYear>
        <noteText>Multiple US police departments (including LAPD&apos;s PredPol/PredCrime deployment) discontinued place-based and person-based predictive policing programs following independent audits and investigative reporting finding the underlying arrest data reproduced historical over-policing of minority neighbourhoods, treating past policing intensity as a proxy for future criminality prior to any individualised suspicion.</noteText>
    </owl:NamedIndividual>
    


    <!-- https://w3id.org/aief/AIAAIC_073 -->


    <owl:NamedIndividual rdf:about="https://w3id.org/aief/AIAAIC_073">
        <rdf:type rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/aief/Incident"/>
        <demonstratesRisk rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/aief/Discrimination"/>
        <impactsRight rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/aief/Art13_FreedomOfArtsScience"/>
        <impactsRight rdf:resource="https://w3id.org/aief/Art21_NonDiscrimination"/>
        <aiCapability>Reasoning (NLP Classification)</aiCapability>
        <incidentID>AIAAIC-073</incidentID>
        <incidentTitle>AI-Detection Tools Falsely Flag Non-Native English Academic Writing</incidentTitle>
        <incidentYear>2023</incidentYear>
        <noteText>Liang et al. (2023, arXiv:2304.02819) found widely-deployed GPT-detection tools misclassified over half of TOEFL essays by non-native English writers as AI-generated, with universities using such tools for academic-integrity enforcement; students and researchers face restricted ability to pursue academic work under suspicion generated by an unaudited classifier, directly implicating the freedom to conduct academic research (Art 13).</noteText>
    </owl:NamedIndividual>
    


